feat(scripts): one command brings the stack up from a cold machine

`make innsegl-up-here` already does the work and this does not repeat it -- it
calls it. What it adds is everything around that call which otherwise has to be
remembered.

Docker Desktop may not be running, and the make target then fails with a daemon
error that says nothing about starting it. `up -d` returns when the containers
are CREATED, not when they answer, so opening the dashboard a second later shows
a load failure that looks like a bug and is a race. And nothing printed where
the dashboard was.

So: start Docker and wait for it, boot through the Makefile, wait on container
HEALTH rather than existence, then report every service with what it is for.

It also prints the transparency log's entry count. A restart that lost the tlog
pin shows up there as a small number, instead of as verification failing days
later for a reason nobody connects to a restart (#176, #219).

The header carries the rule the script exists to enforce: never bring a service
up with `-f deploy/compose/innsegl.yml` alone. The stack is two files and the
second carries the /projects mount and the repository list. Measured 2026-09-13:
recreating the dashboard with one -f also recreated innsegl-api without them,
and "Verify a commit" answered 404 for every commit until the make target put it
back. A single entry point is what stops that recurring.

Verified from a genuinely cold machine, Docker stopped: started it, brought both
files up, reported seven services healthy after ~9s and the log at 555 entries.

No test drove this. It is an operator entry point with no behaviour of its own
-- every gate it runs is one the Makefile already owns, and the useful check was
running it with Docker off, which is what was done.

